/*--------------------------------*- C++ -*----------------------------------*\
  VMFL003.  Manual p. 19: Density = 1.225 kg/m3, Viscosity = 1.7894e-5 kg/m-s.
  simpleFoam is incompressible and wants the KINEMATIC viscosity:

      nu = mu / rho = 1.7894e-5 / 1.225 = 1.4607346938775508e-05 m2/s

  DERIVED FROM THE MANUAL'S OWN MATERIAL PROPERTIES BLOCK -- nothing assumed.
  The same two numbers give Re = rho U D / mu = 13691.740248127866, which the
  manual states as "1.37 X 10^4" (p. 19): consistent to 0.06 %.

  Incompressibility is a declared modelling choice and it is justified here:
  U = 50 m/s in air gives Ma ~ 0.147 and the whole pressure drop (~21.7 kPa) is
  ~21 % of one atmosphere, so the density variation along the pipe would be a
  few per cent under an ideal-gas treatment.  The manual gives a CONSTANT
  density of 1.225 kg/m3 in its own Material Properties block, so a constant-
  density solver is the FAITHFUL reading of the case as specified, and the
  manual's own analytical target dp = f (L/D) rho U^2 / 2 is itself a
  constant-density formula.
\*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*/
FoamFile
{
    version     2.0;
    format      ascii;
    class       dictionary;
    object      transportProperties;
}
// * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * //

transportModel  Newtonian;

nu              1.4607346938775508e-05;

// ************************************************************************* //
